<script type="application/json" id="yd-data-sheets">{% raw %}{"pain_keys":{"Costs them time":"time","Hard on the body or health":"body","Paying a service too much":"service","Service is unreliable":"unreliable","Safety: ice, slopes, heat":"safety","Away from home a lot":"away","Wants it done before they wake up":"wake","Wants a tidy property without the work":"tidy","Old machine broke or is worn out":"old","Deadline: season or an event":"deadline"},"check_intro":"Can I ask a couple of things about your place, so I know it'll work for you?","opener_tail":"What got you interested in this one?","sheets":{"snow":{"label":"Yarbo snow blower","pitch":"So this is a snow blower that runs itself. It heads out while it's still snowing, clears the driveway a pass at a time, and drives back to its dock when it needs a charge.","benefits":[{"say":"You get to stay inside. It's out there clearing while the storm's still going, so nobody has to shovel.","pains":["body","safety","time","wake"]},{"say":"When the battery runs low it goes back to the dock, charges up and carries on where it stopped.","pains":["away","time","wake"]},{"say":"It's a two-stage blower. It clears a path 24 inches wide, takes in snow up to 12 inches deep, and throws it anywhere from 6 to 40 feet. You aim the chute remotely.","pains":["old","tidy"]},{"say":"It's yours, so you're never waiting on a plow service to turn up.","pains":["service","unreliable"]},{"say":"The same robot can mow and blow leaves if you add those attachments, so it's useful the rest of the year too.","pains":["service","tidy","time"]}],"how":["There are two parts. The Core is the robot itself, on tracks, with the battery. The snow blower bolts onto the front.","You show it the driveway once by driving it around the edge in the app. There are no wires to bury.","It finds its way by satellite. The Y40 comes with its own base station, which Yarbo calls the Data Center. The Y40P gets its signal over the internet instead.","In a storm it goes out and clears in passes. A charge lasts about 90 minutes, then it docks, takes about 90 minutes to get from 20% back to 80%, and goes out again.","One charge covers up to 6,000 square feet on the Y40 and 7,000 on the Y40P. That's measured in an inch of snow, so deeper snow means less ground per charge."],"faqs":[["How deep can it handle?","The opening is 12 inches tall. The way it's meant to work is to go out during the storm and keep on top of it. Owners on Yarbo's site say about 6 inches per pass is where it's comfortable. If you're hoping to clear a foot in one go after the storm, it won't do that."],["Does it work on gravel?","Yarbo lists paved surfaces, concrete, asphalt, brick and stone. On packed gravel you run it with the intake raised about 2 inches, so it leaves a thin layer and can still pick up the odd stone. If yours is loose gravel, let me check with Yarbo before I say yes."],["What about ice?","It doesn't melt or break ice, and a thin glaze can make the tracks slip. If your drive ices up or has a slope, the track studs help."],["How steep a driveway?","For snow, Yarbo rates it up to 20 degrees, which is a 36% grade. You might have seen 70% somewhere. That number is for mowing."],["What about the ridge the street plow leaves?","I won't promise it gets through that by itself. Owners deal with it by running the robot often, and by steering it with the remote at the end of the drive."],["Wet, heavy snow?","Heavy wet snow can clog the chute. Yarbo suggests the plow blade for those days."],["How cold can it run?","It's rated down to 13 below zero Fahrenheit, and up to 113."],["Is it hard to set up?","You do the setup yourself. You fit the antennas and the battery, bolt on the chute, put the dock near an outdoor outlet with open sky above it, and then drive the edge of the driveway in the app. The snow blower part weighs 80 to 83 pounds, so think about whether you can lift that or have someone to help. We'll stay on the phone with you while you do it."],["What upkeep does it need?","The tracks need grease about every two weeks through the winter. It also has shear pins, which are meant to snap to protect the machine if it hits something, so it's worth keeping spares."],["Is there a subscription?","The Y40 uses its own base station, so it doesn't need one to find its way. The Y40P uses a satellite correction service called NetRTK. That's free, but it needs internet and coverage where you live. Away from your Wi-Fi it uses 4G, which is free at first and then an optional paid plan."],["How loud is it?","It's electric, so there's no engine. Yarbo says about 60 decibels for the robot on its own, but they haven't published a number for when it's blowing snow, so I can't give you one."]],"compare":"Y40 or Y40P: the Y40P moves faster (1.2 m/s against 0.6), covers about 15% more per charge and needs no base station. It does not climb steeper, throw further or clear deeper. The Y40 has been out long enough to have owner reviews. The Y40P launched in September 2026 and has none yet. Tell the caller that.","checks":["s_surface","s_area","s_slope","s_storm","s_ridge","s_outlet"]},"lawn":{"label":"Yarbo robot mower","pitch":"So this is a robot mower built for big lawns. It mows by itself and finds its way by satellite, so there's no wire to bury around the edge. When the battery's low it drives back to the dock.","benefits":[{"say":"It mows without you, so you get those hours back every week.","pains":["time","tidy","away"]},{"say":"Nobody has to be out on a mower in the heat or on a hill. Yarbo rates it for slopes up to 35 degrees.","pains":["body","safety"]},{"say":"It can handle a lot of grass. The Y40 does up to 6 acres a week and the Y40P up to 7, mowing twice a week.","pains":["service","old"]},{"say":"There's no boundary wire. You drive it around the edge once in the app and it remembers.","pains":["tidy","old"]},{"say":"It's yours, so you're not paying a mowing service or waiting for them to show up.","pains":["service","unreliable"]},{"say":"The same robot takes a snow blower and a leaf blower, so it works for you all year.","pains":["service","time"]}],"how":["There are two parts. The Core is the robot itself, on tracks, with the battery. The mower deck attaches to it.","You show it the lawn once by driving it around the edge in the app.","It cuts a 20 inch strip, and you set the height anywhere from 0.8 to 4 inches. A charge lasts about two hours.","On one charge the Y40 covers about a quarter of an acre and the Y40P a bit more, about 0.3. Then it docks, charges and picks up where it stopped."],"faqs":[["How big a lawn can it do?","Yarbo rates the Y40 for up to 6 acres a week and the Y40P for up to 7, if it mows twice a week. More than 7 acres is too much for one robot."],["How steep can it go?","Up to 35 degrees, which is a 70% grade. If you have banks steeper than that, we have a remote-control mower that's rated up to 50 degrees."],["Does it need a boundary wire?","No. It uses satellite positioning and cameras."],["What about trees?","Heavy tree cover weakens the satellite signal. Tell me how much of the lawn is under trees and I'll tell you what I think."],["Narrow gates or steps between areas?","Yarbo hasn't published how narrow a gap it can get through, and it only clears bumps of about 2 inches. I'd want to check that with Yarbo for your yard before you buy."],["Does it do the edges?","For the edges along fences and flower beds you'd want the trimmer attachment. Yarbo is taking pre-orders for that and estimates January 2027. They say it's still in field testing."],["Is it hard to set up?","You do the setup yourself: antennas, battery, the dock near an outdoor outlet with open sky, and then you drive the edge of the lawn in the app. We'll stay on the phone with you while you do it."]],"compare":"Y40 or Y40P: the Y40 covers up to 6 acres a week, the Y40P up to 7, and it moves faster. A lawn of 3 to 6 acres fits the Y40, and the Y40P gives more room in spring when the grass grows fast. If the lawn is under a quarter acre and they only want mowing, tell them a robot may be more than they need.","checks":["m_acres","m_slope","m_canopy","m_gates","s_outlet"]},"leaves":{"label":"Yarbo leaf blower","pitch":"So this is a leaf blower that drives itself. It works its way around the yard and blows the leaves off the lawn and the driveway, into the beds, the woods or a pile.","benefits":[{"say":"It clears the leaves without you, so you're not out with a blower every weekend in the fall.","pains":["time","body","tidy"]},{"say":"It moves a lot of air. Yarbo rates it at 174 miles an hour and 607 CFM.","pains":["old"]},{"say":"The same robot takes a mower and a snow blower, so one machine covers the whole year.","pains":["service","time"]}],"how":["There are two parts. The Core is the robot itself, and the blower attaches to it.","You show it the area once in the app.","A charge lasts about 70 minutes, then it goes back to the dock."],"faqs":[["Does it pick the leaves up?","No, it blows them where you want them. It doesn't collect or bag or mulch. If you want them bagged, our remote-control mower has a bag and a leaf vacuum mode."],["Can it handle slopes?","Up to 35 degrees."],["Heavily wooded lot?","Heavy tree cover weakens the satellite signal it steers by. On a very wooded lot I'd want to check it'll hold before you buy."]],"compare":"Y40 or Y40P: the blower is the same and the Y40P Core is faster. The Y40P needs NetRTK coverage at the address.","checks":["l_goal","l_canopy","s_outlet"]},"yard":{"label":"Yarbo multi-season system","pitch":"So this is one robot that works all year. You swap the attachment with the season: a mower for summer, a blower for the leaves, and a snow blower for winter.","benefits":[{"say":"One machine does the mowing, the leaves and the snow, and it does them without you.","pains":["time","tidy","service"]},{"say":"You buy the robot once. Each job is an attachment you put on.","pains":["service","old"]},{"say":"Nobody has to be out in the heat, on a hill or in a snowstorm doing it by hand.","pains":["body","safety","wake"]},{"say":"It's yours, so you're not waiting on a service at any time of year.","pains":["unreliable","service","away"]}],"how":["The Core is the robot itself, on tracks, with the battery. Each attachment bolts onto it.","You show it each area once in the app. There's no boundary wire.","As a mower it cuts a 20 inch strip and does up to 6 acres a week on the Y40, or 7 on the Y40P. As a snow blower it clears a 24 inch path and does 6,000 or 7,000 square feet per charge in an inch of snow. As a leaf blower it's rated at 174 miles an hour.","It goes back to the dock and charges by itself between runs."],"faqs":[["How hard is it to swap attachments?","The snow blower weighs 80 to 83 pounds, so think about whether you can lift or roll that, or have someone to help."],["Is the slope limit the same for every job?","No, and it's worth knowing. For mowing and leaves it's 35 degrees. For snow it's 20 degrees."],["Can I start with one attachment and add more later?","Yes. If you want to spend less now, leave out the one you need least and add it later."],["Is the bundle cheaper than buying the pieces?","Sometimes the pieces come out cheaper. I'll show you both totals and quote you the lower one."]],"compare":"Pick the Core by the bigger job. Go to the Y40P when the lawn is 6 acres or more, or the snow area is over 6,000 square feet. Otherwise the Y40.","checks":["m_acres","m_slope","s_area","s_slope","s_surface","s_outlet"]},"core":{"label":"Yarbo Core","pitch":"So the Core is the robot itself: the body on tracks, with the battery and the computer. It doesn't do any yard work until you add an attachment for the job, like the mower or the snow blower.","benefits":[{"say":"You buy the Core once and add an attachment for each job you want done.","pains":["service","old"]},{"say":"It finds its way by satellite, so there's no boundary wire.","pains":["tidy"]}],"how":["The Y40 comes with a Data Center, which is its own base station. That needs open sky and a cable run to your router. A 30 metre cable comes in the box.","The Y40P has no base station. It gets its signal through NetRTK, which needs internet and coverage where you live.","Every Core comes with a dock."],"faqs":[["Which attachments fit?","The mower, the snow blower and the leaf blower are the same hardware for both Cores. We'll quote you the listing that matches yours."],["Do I need anything else?","You need an attachment for the job. Tell me what you want it to do and I'll price the Core and the attachment together, whichever way works out cheaper."]],"compare":"The Y40P moves faster (1.2 m/s against 0.6) and covers about 15% more per charge. It does not climb steeper, throw further or clear deeper. The Y40 has owner reviews. The Y40P launched in September 2026.","checks":["s_outlet","s_net"]},"trimmer":{"label":"Yarbo Trimmer Package","pitch":"So this is the edging attachment for a Yarbo. It trims along fences and flower beds, the places the mower deck can't reach.","must":"Before we go further, you should know Yarbo is taking pre-orders for this, with delivery estimated for January 2027. They say it's still in field testing.","benefits":[{"say":"It tidies up the edges the mower leaves, so you're not walking the fence line with a string trimmer.","pains":["tidy","time","body"]}],"how":["It attaches with a mount that comes in the package. Do not add a second mount to the order.","The one listing fits both Cores."],"faqs":[["When does it ship?","Yarbo's estimate is January 2027. It's a pre-order and still in field testing, so I can't promise anything earlier."]],"compare":"","checks":[]},"mowrator":{"label":"Mowrator S1 remote-control mower","pitch":"So this is a mower you drive with a remote control. It doesn't mow by itself. You stand somewhere safe and flat and steer it, which is why people buy it for steep banks and rough ground.","benefits":[{"say":"You stay off the slope. You stand on flat ground and drive it from there.","pains":["safety","body"]},{"say":"It's made for steep ground. The 2WD is rated to 24 degrees, and the Grip Tread edition goes up to 50.","pains":["safety","old","service"]},{"say":"It cuts a 21 inch strip and covers about half an acre an hour.","pains":["time","tidy"]},{"say":"There's nothing to set up. You charge the battery and mow. No app, no mapping, no gas.","pains":["old","time"]},{"say":"It's yours, so that bank gets cut when you want it cut.","pains":["service","unreliable"]}],"how":["It runs on a 56 volt battery. The 12Ah battery does up to about three quarters of an acre per charge, and the 18Ah about an acre and an eighth. Tall, overgrown grass uses it up faster.","There are four editions: 2WD, 4WD, 4WD Wide Wheels for soft or wet ground, and 4WD Grip Tread for the steepest slopes.","With the bag and the mulching blades it can also vacuum up leaves."],"faqs":[["Does it mow by itself?","No, you drive it with the remote the whole time. If you want something that mows without you, that's the Yarbo."],["Which edition for my slope?","Mowrator rates the 2WD to 24 degrees, the 4WD to 37, the Wide Wheels to 40 and the Grip Tread to 50. What's the steepest part you mow?"],["Can I upgrade the wheels later?","You can, but buying the edition you need costs less than buying a base 4WD and adding the wheels afterwards."],["Can it plow snow?","With the Snow Plow Kit, yes, on the 4WD editions. It pushes the snow, it doesn't blow it. It handles about 4 inches, on slopes up to 20 degrees. The kit is a pre-order right now."],["How long does the battery last?","About three quarters of an acre on the 12Ah and an acre and an eighth on the 18Ah. If you mow more than that in one go, a spare battery covers it."],["Can you match a lower price?","Mowrator sets the lowest price its dealers can sell at, so I can't go under it."]],"compare":"Mowrator or Yarbo: the Mowrator suits any part steeper than 35 degrees, rough or overgrown ground, heavy tree cover, or a caller who doesn't want to set up an app. The Yarbo suits a caller who wants their time back, with slopes inside 35 degrees.","checks":["w_slope","w_ground","w_area","w_snow"]},"pool":{"label":"Robotic pool cleaner","pitch":"So this is a cordless robot that cleans the pool for you. You lower it in, it scrubs and vacuums on its own battery, and there's no hose or cable in the water.","benefits":[{"say":"It does the vacuuming and scrubbing, so you're not standing at the edge with a pole.","pains":["time","body","tidy"]},{"say":"It's cordless, so there's no cable to tangle or trip over.","pains":["old","tidy"]},{"say":"You don't have to pay a pool service for the routine cleaning.","pains":["service","unreliable"]},{"say":"Each model is rated for a certain pool size, so we can match one to yours.","pains":["old"]}],"how":["It runs on its own battery and collects the dirt in a basket you empty.","Some models only do the floor, and those are for flat-bottomed pools. The others also climb the walls and scrub the waterline.","You still need the pump, the filter and the chemicals. The robot handles the cleaning."],"faqs":[["Will it climb the walls?","That depends on the model. The WYBOT A1 and the Aiper Scuba 800 only do the floor. The others do the floor, the walls and the waterline."],["Does it pick up leaves floating on top?","Only the Aiper Scuba X1 Pro Max cleans the surface as well. With any of the others, you'd want a skimmer for what floats."],["Can I try it in my pool and send it back?","Yes. You can return it within 30 days of delivery, and there's no restocking fee. You'd pay the return shipping back to the supplier, so let's make sure it's the right one before you order."],["Is it OK in a salt water pool?","Tell me your salt level and I'll check it against the model for you."],["What about algae?","If there's a lot of algae, treat the water first. The robot is for keeping a clean pool clean."],["Can I swim while it runs?","No, take it out before anyone gets in."],["Who ships it and handles the warranty?","WYBOT ships theirs to you directly and handles any warranty claim."]],"compare":"Most home pools are small, so size rarely decides it. What decides it is whether they want the walls done, how fine the dirt is, how hands-off they want to be, and budget. Check the area and the longest side against the model's rating.","checks":["p_state","p_pobox","p_area","p_floor","p_clean","p_surface_debris"]},"skimmer":{"label":"Robotic pool skimmer","pitch":"So this is a little robot that floats on top of the pool and collects leaves, bugs and pollen before they sink. That keeps it off the floor and saves you skimming by hand.","benefits":[{"say":"It catches things on the surface before they sink to the bottom.","pains":["tidy","time"]},{"say":"It charges from the sun, so most days there's nothing to plug in.","pains":["time","away"]},{"say":"You're not out there with a net every morning.","pains":["body","time","wake"]}],"how":["It floats around the pool by itself and collects into a basket you empty.","It charges from sunlight. Some models also come with a wall charger for shade and cloudy weeks."],"faqs":[["Will it work on an infinity-edge pool?","The Betta Flex is the only one we carry that's rated for infinity edges."],["My pool is shaded. Is that a problem?","Go for one with a wall charger, the Betta SE Plus or the Betta Neo. A solar-only skimmer won't keep up in a fully shaded or indoor pool."],["Can I control it from my phone?","The Betta Neo, the Aiper EcoSurfer M2 and the WYBOT F1 all have an app."],["Can you match a lower price on Betta?","Betta sets the lowest price its dealers can sell at, so I can't go under it."],["Does it clean the floor too?","No, it works the surface. For the floor and walls you'd want a pool robot."]],"compare":"The Betta SE Plus suits most pools. Flex for infinity edges, Neo for phone control. The WYBOT F1 is the lowest price and comes bundled with a WYBOT robot.","checks":["p_state","p_pobox","k_pool","k_want"]},"windows":{"label":"HOBOT window cleaning robot","pitch":"So this is a robot that sticks to the glass by suction and cleans the window while you stay on the ground. It's tied off with a safety rope the whole time.","benefits":[{"say":"Nobody has to go up a ladder or lean out of a window.","pains":["safety","body"]},{"say":"It cleans the pane while you get on with something else.","pains":["time","tidy"]},{"say":"You don't have to book and pay a window cleaner each time.","pains":["service","unreliable"]}],"how":["It plugs into a wall outlet and holds on by suction. You tie the safety rope to something solid indoors.","If the power cuts out, a backup battery keeps it on the glass for about 20 minutes.","It needs a rectangular pane at least 16 by 16 inches, and the glass has to be smooth and in good condition."],"faqs":[["Will it work on frameless glass, like railings or a shower screen?","For that you'd want the HOBOT-2S, which can sense the edge of the glass. The 298 is for framed windows."],["Frosted or textured glass?","No, it needs smooth glass to hold on."],["Does it run on battery?","No, it runs off a wall outlet, so you need one within about 13 feet. The battery is only there as a backup."],["Does it get into the corners?","HOBOT says the 2S reaches about 80% of each corner."]],"compare":"HOBOT-2S for frameless glass or the best corner reach. HOBOT-298 for framed windows when price matters most.","checks":["h_glass","h_pane","h_smooth","h_outlet"]},"floors":{"label":"HOBOT LEGEE-688","pitch":"So this is a robot that vacuums and mops hard floors in the same pass.","benefits":[{"say":"It vacuums and mops together, so the floors get done without you doing either.","pains":["time","body","tidy"]}],"how":["It's for hard floors, up to about 1,600 square feet."],"faqs":[["Does it work on carpet?","It's made for hard floors. If your home is mostly carpet, let me check before you buy."]],"compare":"","checks":["f_floor","f_area"]},"part":{"label":"Part or accessory","pitch":"Let me pull that one up and tell you what it is and what it fits.","benefits":[],"how":["Read from the product page below. If the page does not say what it fits, find out and call back. Never guess a fit."],"faqs":[],"compare":"","checks":[]}}}{% endraw %}</script>
